From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Roman Leventov <leventov.ru@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 04:01:45 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 23/25] local_backend: build with bounded jemalloc

Select jemalloc by default for local backend targets where the bundled allocator is supported, while retaining no-default-features builds as a system-allocator control. On GNU/Linux, enable background purging, use 32 automatic arenas by default, accept effective startup overrides from 1 through 128, require fatal handling for invalid settings, and keep heap profiling disabled and inactive. Validate the effective Linux configuration at startup and reject the glibc-only trim path.

Install jemalloc as the Rust global allocator and force its process-wide allocator entry-point override on supported targets. Export allocator identity, availability, effective configuration, total initialized arena count, and family-specific byte gauges. Keep mallinfo2, malloc_info, and malloc_trim exclusive to GNU/Linux no-jemalloc builds; other no-jemalloc targets use the system allocator availability contract.

Document the platform-specific allocator contracts and include statistics and profiling support without enabling profiling.

A fresh self-hosted process reported the intended 32 automatic arenas, 33 initialized arenas in total, a 10-second dirty-page decay, and active background purging. After about 14 minutes it used 9.52 GiB process RSS and 10.04 GiB of cgroup memory, with 1.56 GiB allocated, 2.04 GiB allocator-resident, 3.15 GiB retained address space, and about 20 GiB of cgroup headroom. No OOM, cgroup-limit, reclamation, or shedding events occurred during that interval.

This is an initial fresh-process observation, not evidence of long-duration allocator dominance. Jemalloc retained address space is also not directly comparable with glibc's logically free arena space.
